 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to enacting the \"toll payer protection act\" which includes a toll by mail system for the payment of tolls by electronic toll devices; provides for the repeal of such system five years after the enactment of such system.",
 "title": "Relates to enacting the \"toll payer protection act\"",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A7587",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A7587-2019",
     "printNo": "A7587",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 201\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 7587-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to enacting\n      the  \"toll  payer protection act\"; and providing for the repeal of\n      certain provisions upon expiration thereof\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This legislation makes a number of substantial and systemically  prob-\nlematic  changes  to  the capability of toll authorities to both collect\ntolls and enforce toll violations within the state of New  York  to  the\ndetriment  of  the end users of the system, and the efficient and neces-\nsary operation of these authorities. I  vetoed  similar  legislation  in\n2018, and the current legislation contains the same shortcomings.\n \n  Open  road,  cashless  tolling  is  critical in modernizing New York's\nroadways, easing congestion, and reimagining our  transportation  system\nfor  the 21st century economy. Transitioning the State to cashless toll-\ning not only improves the safety of motorists,it  reduces  carbon  emis-\nsions  and  helps  further  the State's efforts to provide a greener and\nmore modern New York for all.\n \n  As noted previously, the Thruway Authority, MTA Bridges  and  Tunnels,\nand  the  Port  Authority  of  New  York and New Jersey continue to take\nsignificant actions to ensure a fair and balanced approach to toll bill-\ning and collection.\n \n  The legislation in question, however, lowers to the point of insignif-\nicance any penalty that could be charged for nonpayment of a  toll  bill\nand  prohibits  registration suspension for nonpayment, hamstringing the\nState's enforcement ability. Fairness for all users  demands  that  toll\nevaders who flout the law pay their fair share. Instead, this bill would\nfacilitate greater toll evasion, putting a greater financial onus on law\nabiding  users, which is untenable. We should be facilitating toll payer\nequity, not abolishing it.\n \n  Further, the State Department of Motor Vehicles and the aforementioned\nauthorities have signed a reciprocity agreement with  Massachusetts  and\nmore  of  these  agreements must be executed to ensure toll payer equity\nacross the region. This legislation, however, would effectively undercut\nthis agreement and prevent any future agreements from going into effect.\n \n  To help further ensure a fair and efficient system, I have  instructed\nthe  aforementioned  authorities  to  create and implement a \"toll payer\nadvocate\" which will be tasked with assisting drivers  that  are  having\ndifficulty navigating the toll and fine payment process. For the reasons\nstated above, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 201,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2019
   }
